== The Fourth Sermon

#quote[He will destroy on this mountain the face of the covering cast over all people, and the veil that is spread over all nations. He will swallow up death in victory.—Isa 25:7, 8.]

We have already spoken at length of the spiritual and eternal favors of God, set out in the former verse, “On this mountain the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts will make a feast of fat things.” While our soul is in the body, it is much guided by our imagination. Spiritual things are therefore presented by outward things, and conveyed to the soul that way; only we must remember that there is a far greater excellency in the things themselves than in their representation. For what are all banquets, fatness with marrow, wine on the lees, to the joy and sweetness of religion, begun here, and accomplished in the world to come?

In Christ there is nothing but marrow and sweetness in religion, which may refresh a man in the lowest condition, if he can only have a taste of it.

Now because the spiritual things of Christ do us no good as long as they are hidden, the Holy Spirit therefore sets down a promise, “that God will take away the covering cast on all people, and the veil spread over all nations.”

But there are some things that will dampen all mirth. Now here is security against them, so that our joy may be complete; and this is in the next verse, to which I now come, “He will swallow up death in victory; He will wipe away tears from all faces.” The prophet, having spoken of a great feast before, an excellent feast, sets forth here the courses of that feast. What is it that accompanies it?

First of all, there will be light to reveal the excellency of the feast; the veil is taken away, and a knowledge is given to know divine things in a spiritual manner.

Then the fear of death, which will dampen all feasts, is taken away. “He will swallow up death in victory, and wipe away all tears,” that is, all sorrow. The effect is put for the cause. This is an excellent promise, an excellent course in this spiritual banquet. Suppose a man were set at a feast furnished with all delicacies, royally attended, his clothes suitable, and had a sword hung over his head ready to fall upon him; it would put such a damper on his spirit as would spoil the joy of this feast. So to hear of spiritual excellencies, and yet death, and hell, and damnation coming along, alas! where is the comfort you speak of? And therefore, to make the feast more perfect, there is not only light and knowledge, but the removal of whatever may dampen the feast. So this must come in to comfort all the rest. “He will swallow up death in victory, and wipe away tears from all faces.” Death is here represented to us under the word victory, as a combatant, as one that we are to fight with, a captain.

And then here is the victory over him: Christ overcomes him, and overcomes him gloriously. It is not only a conquest, but a swallowing of him up. Usually God treats all sorts of enemies in their own kind. He causes those who spoil to be spoiled, those who swallow up to be swallowed up. So death, the great swallower, will be swallowed up.

Beloved, death is the great king of kings, and the emperor of emperors, the great captain and ruling king of the world; for no king has such dominion as death has. It spreads its government and victory over all nations. He is impartial, though a tyrant. As a tyrant spares none, he is impartial in this. He subdues young and old, poor and rich. He levels scepters and spades together. He levels all. There is no difference between the dust of an emperor and the humblest man. He is a tyrant who governs over all. And so there is this fairness in him: he spares none.

He has continued from the beginning of the world to this time; but he is a tyrant brought in by ourselves, Rom 5:12. Sin let in death. It opened the door. Death is no creature of God’s making. Satan brought in sin, and sin brought in death. So we ourselves are accessories to the powerful stroke of this prevailing tyrant. And therefore sin is called the cause of death. Sin brought in death, and arms death. The weapon that death fights with, and causes great terror with, is sin. The cause is armed with the power of the wrath of God for sin, the fear of hell, and damnation. So wrath, and hell, and damnation arming sin, it brings a sting of itself, and puts a venom into death. All cares, and fears, and sorrows, and sicknesses are lesser and petty deaths, harbingers of death itself; but the attendants that follow this great king are worst of all, as Rev 6:8, “I looked and saw a pale horse. Its rider’s name was Death, and hell followed close behind.” What would death be, if it were not for the pit and dungeon that follows it? So death is attended with hell, and hell with eternity. Therefore here is a strange kind of prevailing. There is no victory where there is no enemy, and therefore death must be an enemy; indeed, it is the worst enemy, and the last enemy. Death is not placed in the front ranks, but it is placed last for the greatest advantage, and is a great enemy. What does death do? It deprives us of all comfort, pleasure, communion with one another in this life, callings, and whatever else is comforting. The grave is the house of oblivion. Death is terrible in itself, even to nature, as Augustine says, where it is not swallowed up by Christ; for it is an evil in itself, and, as I said, armed with a sting of sin, after which follows hell.

Now this death is swallowed up. When the Scripture makes death a person, it is not unfitting for us to speak as the Scripture does. The Scripture makes death a person, and puts a kind of triumphing spirit in God’s children over death. “Where, O death, is your sting? Where, O grave, is your victory?” (1 Cor 15:55). Death is the greatest swallower, and yet it is swallowed up by Christ. Death has swallowed up all, and when it has swallowed them up, it keeps them. It keeps the dust of kings, subjects, great and small, until the general day of judgment, when death will be swallowed up by itself. It is therefore of the nature of those Solomon speaks of, who cry, “Give, give” (Prov 30:15), and it is never satisfied, like the grave. Yet this death is swallowed up in victory.

But how does death come to be swallowed up? Christ will swallow up death in victory, for himself and his own.

Reason. First of all, because sin brought in death, our Savior Christ became sin, a sacrifice to his father’s justice for sin. He was made sin for us, he was made a curse for us, to take away the curse due to us; and sin being taken away, what has death to do with us, and hell, and damnation, the attendants on death? Nothing at all. Therefore, Col 2:14, 15, upon the cross Christ nailed the law, and sin, and the devil. There he reigned over principalities and powers, which were only executioners let loose by reason of our sins. And God being satisfied for sin, the devil has nothing to do with us but to exercise us, and that only for our good. So he has swallowed up death, because by his death he has taken away sin, and so the power of Satan, whose power is by sin. And therefore it is excellently set down, Heb 2:14, “He too shared in their flesh and blood, so that through death He might destroy him who holds the power of death, that is, the devil.” So Christ by death overthrew Satan, who had the power of death, because by death he took away sin, the sins of all, and bore our sins upon the cross, and was made sin for us, he who knew no sin. He is ours, if we believe. For Christ is given to a particular man when he believes. Beloved, Christ upon the cross triumphed over all our spiritual enemies, sin, and death, and all. It was a kingdom of patience. You#super[+] know there is a double kingdom of Christ: a kingdom of patience, and a kingdom of power.

+ Christ on the cross, suffering the punishment due to sin, overcame the law, and the devil, and sin, which is the kingdom of patience.
+ The kingdom of power he has in heaven. If Christ was so able in his kingdom of patience to conquer our greatest enemies, what will he do in his kingdom of power? As Paul reasons, “If by his death we are saved, much more now that he triumphs in heaven, and appears for us, is he able to convey greater things to us” (Rom 5:10).

If Christ in the days of his flesh conquered, how glorious will his conquest be at the day of judgment! Note: Christ has conquered all in his own person, as our head; then he will conquer for us in his mystical body. What is now done in his person will be done in his members. In the meantime, faith is our victory, his conquest over death is our victory; his victory over all our spiritual enemies is our victory. Everyone who believes is a conqueror of death, though he dies, because he sees it conquered in Christ his head; and as it is truly conquered in him, so Christ will conquer it in all his members. For as Christ in his natural body has gone to heaven, there to appear on our behalf, so mystical Christ will be wholly in glory. He will not leave a finger behind. We will all triumph over all our spiritual enemies. As Christ’s natural body is glorious in heaven as our head, so his mystical body will be also.

You#super[+] see, then, how death is swallowed up by Christ as our surety, as the second Adam upon the cross; and truly swallowed up in him. And by faith this victory is ours, and the time will come when in our own persons it will be swallowed up in victory.

This might be enlarged, but I hasten to make use of it.

Mark, I beseech you#super[+], how death is swallowed up by Christ in his own person for our good. He gave way a great deal to death, for death seized on him upon the cross. Death severs soul from body. Death had him in his own private chamber, his grave, for three days. Indeed, this great king and tyrant death had a great conquest over Christ himself. But here was the glory of this victory! When death, this great conqueror of the world, had Christ upon the cross, and in his own dominion, in the grave, where he rules and reigns, consuming and swallowing up all, death was forced to give up all; and Satan thought he would have a great morsel when he devoured Christ, but there was a hook in Christ’s divine power that caught him, so that when he thought to have swallowed up Christ, he was swallowed up himself. His head was then broken. He never had such a blow as he had from Christ on the cross, when he was overcome, being visibly a scorn to the world, yet invisibly, in God’s acceptance of that sacrifice, and in a spirit of faith. Christ triumphs over Satan. Death was subdued even in his own kingdom, and that makes the victory great.

Because death seized on Christ without right, Christ has freed us from the evil of death when it had a right to us. Death has lost all its right by fastening on Christ, and so has become like a drone without a sting. So the great swallower of all is swallowed up itself at last by Christ.

Use 1. Now for a comforting use of it. First, let us consider that God often gives way a great deal to his greatest enemies. God uses a stratagem of retreating; he seems to retreat and give liberty to his enemies, but it is to triumph and trample upon them with greater shame. He will tread them to dust afterward. Christ gave death a great deal of liberty. He was crucified and tormented, then taken to the grave, and there he lay. And this was to raise a greater triumph over this great prevailer, over the world and death itself.

It continues so in the church. Does he not give way to the enemies of the church? They may come to say, Aha, aha, that is how we would have it. Now the poor children of God are where we want them; but then comes sudden destruction. God, to make his victory more glorious, and the more to expose their cruelty, comes upon them when they are at the top of their pleasure, and the church at the bottom of abasement. Then God swallows up all in victory, as Christ did death when it seemed to have been victorious itself.

This is a very comforting consideration, for if death was overcome when it seemed to overcome Christ, what need have we to fear any other enemy? Christ has broken the net, as an eagle or great bird does, and the rest escape by him.

You#super[+] may enlarge on this in your#super[+] own meditations. He will swallow up death in victory. This is said of the time to come: he will swallow up death. But Paul says it is also past, and swallowed up already. Faith says it is done; and so it is in our head. Would it not be comforting now to all true-hearted Christians to hear that the church fares better, and that the enemies were swallowed up, for they are only the instruments of this inferior death? Let us get the spirit of faith, and see them all conquered, for certainly they will have the worst of it at last. He who has swallowed up death in victory will swallow up all that are the cause of death. And therefore the Scripture speaks of these things as past, “Babylon has fallen, like a millstone cast into the bottom of the sea” (Rev 18:21).

Get a spirit of faith, and we will never be much troubled by Babylon; for all the enemies of Christ, and the adherents of that man of sin, must go down, and partake of the judgments threatened in the Revelation. Heaven has decided it, and all the scheming of Rome and hell cannot annul it. They are already swallowed up, to faith, and Christ will rule until he has put them all under his feet, Ps 110:1; which will be done, not only to destroy them, but to raise himself higher in giving them up to their confusion.

Use 2. Again, if death is swallowed up in victory, labor to be one with Christ crucified, for union with him. Begin with union with Christ crucified. The first union is with Christ abased, and then with Christ glorified. And therefore labor to see sin, which brought in death, subdued in some measure by the power of Christ’s death, and then we will have comfort in his death glorified. For in my “holy mountain” death is swallowed up, that is, in the true church of Christ. Labor to be members of Christ; otherwise death will come to assail you#super[+] as a tyrant indeed, armed with a terrible sting, in its full force. It is the most terrible thing to see death come armed with the wrath and anger of God, and attended with hell and damnation. Labor, therefore, to be one with Christ crucified, to get our sins crucified, and ourselves partakers of his death; and then there is no damnation, no fear of death, to those who are in Christ. They may die, but they are freed from eternal death, and they will rise again, even as Christ’s body rose, to glory.

Get, therefore, into Christ, and desire the power of his death subduing sin. In whatever measure we grow in that, we grow in boldness and joy, and in whatever privileges follow Christ.

Use 3. Again, when we are in Christ, true members of him, then let us be thankful to God for this victory, thankful to Jesus Christ who has given us victory. When we think of death, of sin, of judgment, of hell, of damnation, let us be of the frame a Christian should be. Now let the man who has the most terrible and fearful things in the world as conquered enemies say, Oh, blessed be God for Christ, and blessed be Christ for dying for us, and by death disarming death of its sting! So that now we can think of it quietly in our judgments; now we can think of all these as conquered enemies: this is the fruit of Christ’s death. They are not only enemies, but friends in Christ. Sin, the remainder of it—(the guilt of it, which binds over to damnation, is taken away)—the remainders of it serve to humble us, to make us feel the power of pardon, and to desire another world, where we will be wholly spiritual. So death is a part of the estate settled on us as Christ’s bride. “All things are yours … life or death” (1 Cor 3:22). Death does us many excellent services. It is a door and passage to life. Death is the death of itself; it destroys itself. We never truly live until we die, and when we die, we are past the fear of death. So sin dies, misery dies, death dies. Though it takes us from comforts, and employments, and friends here, yet it is a change to a better place, and better company, and better employments, and a better condition, to be in a glorious condition for eternity; and therefore we have cause to bless God in Christ, who took our nature, and in our nature disarmed our greatest enemy, sin, and so disarmed death, and freed us from the wrath of God, and hell, and damnation. Oh, we can never be thankful enough for this!

Use 4. Again, if death is swallowed up in victory, let us be ashamed of the fear of death, because Christ says he will swallow him up, as he has already in his own person. Shall we be afraid of an enemy that is swallowed up in our head, and will be swallowed up in every one of us? If we cherish fear, we show we do not look for a share in this promise; for it is a promise that “on this holy mountain death will be swallowed up in victory,” and why should we fear a conquered enemy? No one will fear an enemy that is conquered.

Objection. But how did Christ come to fear death, and we not to fear it?

Answer. Christ had to deal with death armed with a terrible sting, with sin, and the wrath of God for sin. And therefore, when he was to die, he said, “Father, let this cup pass from Me” (Matt 26:39). But death is disarmed to us. He had to encounter sin and the wrath of God, and death in all its strength. But we do not. We are to deal with death like the bronze serpent, which has the shape of death but no sting at all. It has become a drone ever since it lost its sting in Christ. Life took death, so that death might take life, as one said. The meaning is, Christ’s life itself took death, so that we who were so subject to death, that we were death itself, might take life. Oh, blessed consideration! Nothing is comparable to the consideration of the death of Christ! It is the death of deaths.

And then again, we are sure of victory. It is conquered in our head, and will be in us. But you#super[+] say we are to struggle with the pangs of death, and many troubles meet in death. It is true, but it is conquered to faith, and in Christ our head. We must fight. Christ trains us to overcome death ourselves by faith, and then we are sure of victory. Join these two together. It is conquered in Christ our head, and will be conquered by us. Death keeps our dust, and must give it all back again.

Objection. But in the meantime we die.

Answer. It is so, but we are sure of victory. He who has conquered for us will protect us in our combat. We fight against death and the terror of it in the strength and faith of his victory. Join these three together.

He who has been our Savior in life will be so to death, and not exclusively, as if then to leave us, but to death, and in death, forever; yes, most ready to help us in our last conflict. Indeed, to wicked men death is terrible, for he sends the devil to fetch them out of the world; but for those who are his, he sends his angels to fetch them, and he helps them in their combat. We must not, therefore, fear overmuch. There is a natural fear of death. Death worked upon Christ himself, God-man; not only death, but such a death. He was to be forsaken by his Father, and lie under the sense of the wrath of God; the separation of that soul from the body he took upon him was terrible; and therefore he says, “If it is possible, let this cup pass from Me”; that was nature, and without it he would not have been true man. But what I say is that grace may be above nature. Death is a time of darkness. It strips us of earthly comforts, friends, callings, employments; but then comes the eye of faith to lay hold on the victory of Christ in time to come, when death will be nothing but swallowed up in victory; and then on the glorious state to come, to which death brings us. So here faith must be above sense, and grace above nature; and therefore, I beseech you#super[+], let us labor for it.

There are two sorts of men to whom I would speak a little.

First. Those who in a kind of bravado seem to slight death; men of base spirits, as we call them; fools, vainglorious spirits, empty spirits. Is there any creature, unless he is in Christ, able on good grounds to slight so great an enemy as death, armed with a sting of sin, and attended with hell and damnation? The Romish and devilish spirits are terrible; but if your sins are not pardoned, it is the most terrible thing in the world to die, for there is a gulf afterward. What shall we say, then, of single combatants, who for vainglory are prodigal of their lives, who for a foul word, a little disgrace, will venture on this enemy that is armed with sin; and if they die, they die in sin. And this is the miserable condition of the man who dies in sin: his death opens the gate to another death, which is eternal. They say they have repented, but there is no repenting of a sin yet to be committed. Can you repent of a sin before it is committed? That is only a mockery of God. And what does the Scripture say? Is it not the most terrible judgment under heaven to die in our sins? A man who dies in sin dies in hell: he goes from death to hell, and that eternal.

I wonder, therefore, that the wisdom of flesh and blood should take away men’s wits, and faith, and grace, and all, so far as to slight death, and repentance, as if it were so easy. Now, beloved, death is a terrible thing. It has a sting, and you will know it. If you do not have grace to feel its sting while you live, when you die the sting will revive; then your conscience will awake in hell. Drunkenness and jollity take away the sense of sin; but sin will revive, and conscience will revive. God has not put it into us for nothing. Death is terrible if it is not disarmed beforehand. And if you set about dying without disarming it before, it will not be faced down. It is not an enemy to be scorned and slighted. And therefore be Christians in good earnest, or else give up your#super[+] profession of faith, and perish eternally. For we must all die, and it is a greater matter than we take it to be. But if we are true Christians, it is the sweetest thing in the world, an end of all misery, a beginning of true happiness, an entrance into whatever is comforting. Blessed are those who are in the Lord by faith, and those who die in the Lord. Their death is better than the day of life. Our birthday brings us into misery; and therefore let me speak to true Christians, and bid them be ashamed of fearing death too much, which has turned from an enemy into a reconciled friend.

Second. This may, in the next place, yield great consolation to those who are in Christ Jesus, that death by Christ is swallowed up in victory; and all the more because the Holy Spirit means more than a bare victory over death. Death is not only subdued, but is made a friend to us, as in Ps 110:1 it is said, “his enemies will be his footstool.” Now a footstool is not only trampled upon, but is a help to rise. And so death is not only subdued, but it advances God’s children, and raises them higher. It is not only an enemy, but a reconciled friend; for death does what no friend in the world can do. It ends all our misery, and is the entrance into all happiness for eternity. And whatever it strips us of here, it gives us the advantage of better things in another world. It cuts off our pleasures, and profits, and company, and callings here; but what is that to our blessed change afterward, to our praising of God forever, to the company of blessed souls, and the profits and pleasures at the right hand of God forevermore? And therefore it is not only conquered, but, to show the excellency of his power, he has made it a friend instead of an enemy, and the best friend in the world. It indeed separates soul from body, but it joins the soul to Christ; so the union we have by it is better than the separation, if the union makes us partake of our desire. “I desire to be dissolved,” says St. Paul, Phil 1:23, but that is not well translated. “I desire to depart, and to be with Christ, which is best of all.” So it is not only not an enemy, but a friend. And therefore the apostle makes it our marriage settlement, part of our portion: all things are yours#super[+]. Why? “You#super[+] are Christ’s, and Christ is God’s” (1 Cor 3:23). What are ours? “The present, the future, life, death” (1 Cor 3:22, 23). And well may death be ours, because sin is our enemy; that remainder which is kept in our nature to exercise us, and humble us, and fit us for grace. As Augustine says, I dare be bold to say it is profitable for some to fall, to make them more careful and watchful, and to prize mercy more. So not only death, but sin and the devil himself are ours; for his plots are for our good. God outshoots him with his own bow. “He will give them over to Satan,” says the apostle, “that they may learn not to blaspheme” (1 Tim 1:20). Yet though they have a spirit of blasphemy, by the humbling of their bodies they are taught not to blaspheme; so not only death, but sin, and he who brought sin into the world, the devil, have become our friends.

This being so, it may serve as a special comfort, so that we do not fear the king of fears. The devil has great advantage by this affection of fear when it is set upon this object, death. Overcome death, and all troubles are overcome. Who that has given himself up to God will fear anything? “Skin for skin! A man will give up all he owns in exchange for his life” (Job 2:4). The devil knows that well enough. Therefore “Do not be afraid,” says Christ, “of those who kill the body” (Matt 10:28). Fear causes snares, says Solomon, Prov 29:25, snares of conscience. But if a man has once overcome the fear of death, what more is to be done? What if they take away life? They cannot take away what is better than life, the favor of God. If we die in the Lord, we die in the favor of God, which is better than life; and we will be found in the Lord at the day of judgment, and will be forever with the Lord in heaven. And therefore this is a ground of resolve in good causes, notwithstanding any threats whatever, because death itself is swallowed up in victory.

The worst the world can do is to take away this nature of ours. When they have done that, they have done all they can; and when they have done that, they have done us a favor. That is not to be feared, says Tertullian, which frees us from all that is to be feared. What is to be feared in the world? Every sickness, every disgrace? Why, death frees us from all. We see that every day takes away a piece of one’s life, and when death comes it overthrows itself; for the soul goes at once to the place of happiness. The body sleeps a while, and death has no more power.

“He who believes in Me,” says Christ, “will not see death, but has passed from death to life” (John 5:24). He will not see spiritual death; but as he lives in Christ, he will die in Christ, and rise again in Christ. He who has the life of grace begun will have it brought to completion without interruption. It is a point of wonderful comfort that death is so overcome that we are in heaven already. And it is no hard saying, but agrees with the truth of other points; for are not Christ and we all one? His body is there, and is he not the head of his mystical body? He who carried his natural body there, will he not carry his mystical body there too? Will he be in heaven piecemeal? Therefore we are in heaven already, in the best part of us. We are represented in heaven, for Christ represents us there as the husband does the wife. He has taken possession of heaven for us.

Christ cannot be divided, as Augustine says. “We sit in heavenly places already with Christ” (Eph 2:6). And what a comfort this is, that while we live we are in heaven, and that death cannot hinder us from our resurrection, which is the restoring of all things. And therefore, as the apostle says, “Comfort one another with these things” (1 Thess 4:18). These things indeed have much comfort in them.

Let us labor, then, to be comforted: this is the use the apostle makes of it; and to be fruitful in our places, upon consideration of the victory we have by Christ, 1 Cor 15. It is an excellent chapter that proves at length Christ’s victory as the cause of our victory, because he is the firstfruits that sanctifies all the rest. “Finally, my brothers, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the works of the Lord, because you#super[+] know that your#super[+] labor in the Lord is not in vain.” He raises that exhortation to fruitfulness and constancy from this very ground of the victory Christ has gained by death. “Where, O death, is your sting? Where, O grave, is your victory? Thanks be to God through Jesus Christ.” “And therefore be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, because you#super[+] know that your#super[+] labor in the Lord will not be in vain” (1 Cor 15:58). Make that use the apostle does, of fruitfulness to God for Christ, that we can think of death, and sin, the devil, and all his malice, and not be afraid; indeed, think of them all with comfort, that we are not only freed from their tyranny, but they are our friends.

Christ has the key of hell and death; a saying taken from the custom of governors who carried the key. He has the government and command of hell and death. Now if Christ has command of death, he will not allow death to hurt his members, or triumph over them always. He will keep them in the grave. Our bodies are safe in the grave. The dust is fitted for a heavenly body, for another kind of body than we have now; and Christ, who has the key, will let them out again. Therefore trust a while until the times of restoring come, and then we will have a glorious soul and a glorious body, as the apostle says, 1 Cor 15:43. I beseech you#super[+], think of these things, and get comfort against the evil day. And to that end, be sure to get into Christ, so that we may be in Christ, living and dying, and be found in Christ. For what does the Scripture say? “Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord” (Rev 14:13). It is a proof of blessedness to die for the Lord, but if it is not in the Lord, it is to no purpose. If this happiness of dying for the Lord is granted, it is well; “but blessed are the dead who die in the Lord.” Why? “They will rest from their labors.” Death takes them off from their labors. All their good works go to heaven with them. So says the Spirit, whatever the flesh says. And there is no resting until that time. Their life is full of troubles and encumbrances; and therefore labor to get assurance that we are in Christ, that we may be in Christ, and die in Christ, and then “there is no condemnation for those who are in Christ.”

How stupefied we are to put off preparation for death until it comes! He who forgets Christ, and getting into Christ, all his lifetime, it is God’s just judgment that he should forget himself in death. We see how a villain who has no care for his own life may have power over another man’s life.

And therefore labor to be grafted into Christ by faith; and labor that we may know it by the Spirit of Christ prevailing in us over our natural corruptions more and more. As the apostle says, “There is no condemnation for those who are in Christ”; for the spirit of life, “the law of the Spirit of life which is in Christ has set me free from the law of sin and death” (Rom 8:2), the condemning law of sin. If the law of the spirit of life which is in Christ the head is in us in any measure, it frees us from the condemning law of sin, so that it does not carry us wherever it would. Then we may say with comfort, “There is no condemnation for those who are in Christ”; for the law of the spirit of life in Christ has freed us from the condemning, tyrannizing law of sin and death.

Sin has no law. It is in us as a subdued rebel, but it does not set up a throne. Some hope to be saved by Christ, and yet they set up a throne for sin in the soul. Sin bids them defile themselves, and they must obey it. This is a woeful condition! How can they expect to die in the Lord? Only those can who are freed by the law of the spirit of life. New lords, new laws. When kings conquer, they bring in fundamental laws; and when we are taken from Satan’s kingdom into the kingdom of Christ, the fundamental laws are then altered. Christ by his Spirit sets up a law of believing, and praying, and doing good, and abstaining from evil. The law of the spirit of life frees us from the law of sin and death. I beseech you#super[+], enlarge on these things in your#super[+] thoughts. They are things we must all have use of beforehand, against the evil day. It should be comforting and useful to us all to hear that our enemy, our greatest enemy, death, is swallowed up in victory. And yet there is more comfort in the text.
